					<section class="pol__doc" id="accessibility" aria-labelledby="accessibility-title">
						<header class="pol__doc-head">
							<h2 class="pol__doc-title" id="accessibility-title">
								Accessibility Statement
							</h2>
							<p class="pol__doc-lede">
								Our commitment, what conformance we claim, how it was assessed, and what we
								know is still wrong.
							</p>
							<p class="pol__stamp">Last reviewed {LAST_REVIEWED}</p>
						</header>

						<section class="pol__section" id="accessibility-1">
							<h3 class="pol__h">1. Our commitment</h3>
							<div class="pol__body">
								<p>
									The Itti Foundation is committed to making itti.org.in usable by as many
									people as possible, regardless of disability, device or circumstance.
								</p>
								<p>
									One of the three areas this Foundation works in is neurodivergence. A site
									that is hard to read, that cannot be operated without a mouse, or that
									moves when someone has asked it not to, would contradict its own purpose.
								</p>
							</div>
						</section>

						<section class="pol__section" id="accessibility-2">
							<h3 class="pol__h">2. Conformance status</h3>
							<div class="pol__body">
								<p>
									We target{' '}
									<strong>Web Content Accessibility Guidelines (WCAG) 2.2, Level AA</strong>.
								</p>
								<p>
									This site is <strong>partially conformant</strong> with WCAG 2.2 Level AA:
									most of the standard is met, and the exceptions are listed in section 6. We
									do not claim certification, and the assessment has not been independently
									verified.
								</p>
							</div>
						</section>

						<section class="pol__section" id="accessibility-3">
							<h3 class="pol__h">3. Measures taken</h3>
							<div class="pol__body">
								<p>
									Each of the following was verified against the built site, on every page,
									at viewport widths from a 390-pixel phone to a 2560-pixel display.
								</p>
								<dl class="pol__list">
									<dt>Contrast</dt>
									<dd>
										No text falls below its Level AA threshold: 4.5:1 for normal text and
										3:1 for large text. Three of our four accent colours failed as small
										type and have been darkened for that use; they are unchanged as rules
										and marks.
									</dd>
									<dt>Keyboard operation</dt>
									<dd>
										Every interactive element is reachable and operable by keyboard and
										displays a visible focus indicator. A skip link is the first focusable
										element on every page.
									</dd>
									<dt>Structure and semantics</dt>
									<dd>
										Every page declares its language, carries exactly one first-level
										heading, skips no heading level, and identifies its banner, navigation,
										main content and footer as landmarks.
									</dd>
									<dt>Forms</dt>
									<dd>
										All twenty-eight controls on the registration form have
										programmatically associated labels. Errors are conveyed in text and
										announced to assistive technology, not by colour alone.
									</dd>
									<dt>Images</dt>
									<dd>
										Every image carries alternative text. Artwork in the opening sequence is
										marked decorative, because the adjacent text carries its meaning.
									</dd>
									<dt>Motion</dt>
									<dd>
										Where the operating system requests reduced motion, the opening sequence
										stops animating: no camera movement, no dissolve, no drift. The three
										images still change, so no content is lost; they cut rather than fade.
									</dd>
									<dt>Reflow and zoom</dt>
									<dd>
										No page requires horizontal scrolling at any width in the range tested,
										and content reflows to a single column on small viewports.
									</dd>
									<dt>Operation without scripting</dt>
									<dd>
										Every page renders in full with JavaScript disabled, with one exception
										noted in section 6.
									</dd>
								</dl>
							</div>
						</section>

						<section class="pol__section" id="accessibility-4">
							<h3 class="pol__h">4. Technical specifications</h3>
							<div class="pol__body">
								<p>
									Accessibility relies on HTML, CSS, WAI-ARIA and JavaScript, and on the
									combination of a modern browser with any assistive technology installed on
									the visitor&rsquo;s device.
								</p>
								<p>
									The site is served as static pages over HTTPS. Typefaces are self-hosted,
									and no third-party script runs on any page.
								</p>
							</div>
						</section>

						<section class="pol__section" id="accessibility-5">
							<h3 class="pol__h">5. Assessment approach</h3>
							<div class="pol__body">
								<p>
									The Foundation assessed this site by self-evaluation. Testing combined
									automated checks of the built output with manual keyboard operation,
									contrast measurement against rendered composites rather than nominal
									colours, and inspection with scripting disabled and with reduced motion
									requested.
								</p>
								<p>Self-evaluation is the weakest form of assessment available.</p>
							</div>
						</section>

						<section class="pol__section" id="accessibility-6">
							<h3 class="pol__h">6. Known limitations</h3>
							<div class="pol__body">
								<p>
									Despite our efforts, the following are known to fall short. Each is ours to
									fix.
								</p>
								<dl class="pol__list">
									<dt>No assistive-technology user testing</dt>
									<dd>
										The structure is correct by measurement, which is not the same as being
										good to listen to. We have not yet tested with anyone who uses a screen
										reader daily.
									</dd>
									<dt>No independent audit</dt>
									<dd>
										Every claim on this page was verified by the people who built the site.
									</dd>
									<dt>The account page requires scripting</dt>
									<dd>
										It retrieves your record from our registry as it opens and cannot do so
										with JavaScript disabled. It states this, and gives an address at which
										a person will provide the same information.
									</dd>
									<dt>The opening sequence is animated</dt>
									<dd>
										It is the most demanding element on the site. Reduced motion is the
										alternative we offer; there is no static version of the page.
									</dd>
									<dt>Some navigation leads to unwritten pages</dt>
									<dd>
										Much of the site is not yet written. Those links reach a page that says
										so, which is honest but remains a dead end.
									</dd>
									<dt>English only</dt>
									<dd>
										We ask your preferred language at registration and cannot yet
										correspond in it.
									</dd>
								</dl>
							</div>
						</section>

						<section class="pol__section" id="accessibility-7">
							<h3 class="pol__h">7. Feedback</h3>
							<div class="pol__body">
								<p>
									We welcome reports of accessibility barriers. You do not need to know what
